\section{Related work}
\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Predicting a benchmark score from few items.}
Anchor points~\citep{vivek2024anchor} keep the medoids of clusters of items with similar source responses and average the target's answers on them with cluster-size weights; tinyBenchmarks~\citep{maiapolo2024tiny} fit an item-response-theory (IRT) ability of the target on such anchors; metabench~\citep{kipnis2025metabench} selects items by IRT information and regresses with a generalized additive model.
Others learn a predictor of the target's success on unseen items from a few reference items~\citep{pacchiardi2024100}, an adaptive acquisition policy with a neural predictor~\citep{li2025active}, items that target the model's ability~\citep{hofmann2025fluid,balkir2026confident}, or a predictor of rank correlation to choose subsets~\citep{saranathan-etal-2025-sublime}.
Most are \emph{model-based predictors} with empirical support on held-out models that resemble the sources.
We use the score matrices released with a comparison of such methods~\citep{zhang2025miss} as data; we use neither its code nor its findings.
Item response theory was brought to ML evaluation to describe items and test sets~\citep{lalor-etal-2016-building,martinezplumed2019irt,vania-etal-2021-comparing} and to diagnose leaderboards~\citep{rodriguez2021evaluation}; compact IRT representations of abilities predict performance on new benchmarks~\citep{chen2025compact,shnitzer2023routing}.
Adaptive testing chooses the next item by the current ability estimate~\citep{zhuang2023adaptive}, and amortized IRT predicts item difficulty from content to avoid per-item calibration~\citep{truong2025amortized}; both pick items by information, so inclusion probabilities are unknown and the estimate is model-based.
The closest in spirit to our setting are Sort \& Search~\citep{prabhu2024lifelong}, which ranks test samples by earlier models' results and subselects a subset to estimate accuracy (image classifiers, no design-based correction), and the prediction of larger models' scores from a low-dimensional capability space of many models~\citep{ruan2024observational}, which uses aggregate scores instead of items of the same benchmark.
Benchmark scores are sample means with a non-negligible item-sampling variance~\citep{madaan2024variance,miller2024errorbars}, and predictable from compute~\citep{owen2024predictable}; others reduce cost by dropping items~\citep{perlitz-etal-2024-efficient}.

\paragraph{Estimation with a free covariate.}
Estimating the mean of a finite population from a sample, with an auxiliary variable known for every unit, is the subject of model-assisted survey estimation: generalized-regression and difference estimators use auxiliary predictions to reduce variance and remain design-unbiased~\citep{sarndal1992model}.
Prediction-powered inference~\citep{angelopoulos2023ppi,angelopoulos2023ppiplus} transplants the idea to machine-learning predictions, with a power-tuned weight on the predictions, and active statistical inference~\citep{zrnic2024active} draws the labelled points with probabilities that follow the model's uncertainty.
The latter is the closest to our design and estimator; it differs in the covariate (here a matrix of source-model results on every item, not one model's uncertainty), in the finite binary population with fixed-size designs and a positivity floor, and in the target, which includes the \emph{ordering} of models.
Active testing~\citep{kossen2021active,yilmaz2021sample} selects items by importance sampling with a surrogate trained for one model; prediction-powered ranking~\citep{chatzi2024prediction} ranks LLMs from human preferences; subgroup accuracy~\citep{fogliato-etal-2024-precise} combines direct and regression estimates; and autoevaluation with LLM-judge labels applies the prediction-powered estimator to evaluation without an item design~\citep{boyeau2024autoeval}.
